\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Massive graph Green functions}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Let #{V} be a finite vertex set, with positive edge weights,
and let #{L} be its \vocabk{weighted graph Laplacian}{fdip-0007}.
For #{m>0}, define the \newvocab{massive precision} and its
\newvocab{Green operator} by
##{Q_m=m^2I-L,\qquad G_m=Q_m^{-1}.}
The inverse exists for every such graph by \ref{fdip-00JX},
including disconnected and empty graphs.}

\p{The \newvocab{Green kernel} is defined from the inverse map by
#{G_m(v,w)=(G_m\delta_w)(v)}, where #{\delta_w(v)} is one at
#{v=w} and zero elsewhere. The point-source equations and response
to a general source field are proved in \ref{fdip-00JX}.
The counting and spectral calculations in \ref{fdip-00JW}
realize these operators in explicitly chosen coordinates.}

\p{This convention has to be matched when comparing lattice Green
functions. Wingate's lattice action uses a positive operator built from
minus the backward-forward difference and a mass term
\citet{Section 4, Equations (4.2)-(4.5), p. 3}{wingate2025lattice}.
For unit spacing, that difference term has the sign of our #{-L}.}

\p{In \citet{Theorem 8.26, p. 399}{friedli2017statistical}, the massive
lattice precision is
##{Q=-\frac{1}{2d}\Delta+m^2I.}
Here #{\Delta f(x)=\sum_{y\sim x}(f(y)-f(x))}. The factor #{1/(2d)}
and the lattice boundary conditions belong to that normalization.
Identifying their covariance with an inverse requires that precision,
not an unscaled adjacency Laplacian.}

